From e2cf4ff78f8c17f3152c36a7883667c40534f1bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John KNIPPER <code@jkn.me>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 23:15:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 700/701] aros: link AArch64 programs whose code exceeds BL's
 reach

The AROS link is ld -r (collect-aros): the loader relocates an ELF object
in place, and no final layout ever happens, so lld never adds BL
range-extension veneers. chrome has 355 MiB of code; hundreds of
thousands of its direct branches reach further than +-128 MiB, and the
loader refuses them. The arm64 toolchain now links through aros_link.py,
which runs the usual driver link with a link map and then
aros_veneers.py: islands of "ldr x16, #8; br x16; .quad target" veneers
(the target filled by an ABS64 relocation, which the loader applies), one
near every 64 MiB, and the out-of-reach branches redirected to them.

An island goes only between two input sections that the link map names:
ld -r resolves no reference between input sections, so every reference
that crosses an island is a relocation, which the pass adjusts. A
reference the assembler resolved inside a section (B.cond, CBZ, TBZ,
LDR literal, ADR, ADRP) never crosses one. The pass finds the
displacement collect-aros's second link adds in front of the map's
.text from the symbols both name once, and refuses unless all agree;
it also refuses a symbol spanning an island and an input section aligned
beyond a page. Each island starts with a branch over itself. Smaller
programs are left untouched. AROS_LINK_KEEP_UNVENEERED keeps the link
before the pass, for an independent check.

The relocatable script also folds malloc_hook (absl's LowLevelAlloc,
placed there by ABSL_ATTRIBUTE_SECTION) into .text: as its own code hunk
it was loaded out of its branches' reach.

Checked on the native-TLS chrome: 4 islands, 14 893 veneers; an
independent decoder of the unveneered link found all 5 842 461 resolved
PC-relative words of its code moved together with their targets and
copied unchanged, and every island opening with its branch over.
